-- title:
--   Galois descent: S-fixed basis of a Riemann–Roch space
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a positive natural number and let $S$ be any set of $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`. Work with the field $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldBar M`, the intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the coefficientwise image of `modularFunctionFieldFull M`, the latter being the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the expansions $j(q^{d})$ for the nonzero divisors $d \mid M$. Let $E$ be a divisor on $F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, i.e. a finitely supported integer-valued function on the places of $F/\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and assume that for every $\sigma \in S$ the semilinear automorphism `arithmeticGalois (modularFunctionFieldFull M) σ` — the pair consisting of coefficientwise application of $\sigma$ to Laurent series together with $\sigma$ itself — fixes $E$ under the induced action on divisors. Assume further that $L(E) =$ `riemannRochSpace E`, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subspace of $f \in F$ with $v(f) \le \exp(E(v))$ for all places $v$, is finite-dimensional. Then there is a family $b$ indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}(\dim_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}} L(E))$ of elements of $F$, each lying in $L(E)$, linearly independent over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and with $b_i$ fixed by `arithmeticGalois (modularFunctionFieldFull M) σ` for every index $i$ and every $\sigma \in S$. By the dimension count such a family is a basis of $L(E)$, although the conclusion is phrased as a family of elements of $F$ rather than as a basis of the submodule.
--
--   This is Galois descent for the Riemann–Roch space of an $S$-stable divisor on the base-changed modular curve of level $M$: the space of functions with poles bounded by $E$ admits a basis of functions with coefficients fixed by all $\sigma \in S$. It is the source of rationality statements downstream, being used to produce functions in Riemann–Roch spaces with prescribed residues and inertia- or Galois-invariance, and to obtain rational families of embedding bases.
